2026-06-30 15:43:23,937 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-8adac542-a6ce-4b21-b3f0-42a4a2a38a94', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\n20171003_F1_RM_WJP_0109_FOW_TiO2_F1.raw\n20171003_F1_RM_WJP_0109_FOW_TiO2_F2.raw\n20171003_F1_RM_WJP_0109_FOW_TiO2_F3.raw\n20171003_F1_RM_WJP_0109_FOW_TiO2_F4.raw\n20171003_F1_RM_WJP_0109_REV_TiO2_F1.raw\n20171003_F1_RM_WJP_0109_REV_TiO2_F2.raw\n20171003_F1_RM_WJP_0109_REV_TiO2_F3.raw\n20171003_F1_RM_WJP_0109_REV_TiO2_F4.raw\n20171009_F1_RM_WJP_0109_FOW_TiO2_MS3_F1.raw\n20171009_F1_RM_WJP_0109_FOW_TiO2_MS3_F2.raw\n20171009_F1_RM_WJP_0109_FOW_TiO2_MS3_F3.raw\n20171009_F1_RM_WJP_0109_FOW_TiO2_MS3_F4.raw\n20171009_F1_RM_WJP_0109_REV_TiO2_MS3_F1.raw\n20171009_F1_RM_WJP_0109_REV_TiO2_MS3_F2.raw\n20171009_F1_RM_WJP_0109_REV_TiO2_MS3_F3.raw\n20171009_F1_RM_WJP_0109_REV_TiO2_MS3_F4.raw\n20171013_F1_RM_WJP_0109_FOW_TiO2_ETDandMS3_F1.raw\n20171013_F1_RM_WJP_0109_FOW_TiO2_ETDandMS3_F2.raw\n20171013_F1_RM_WJP_0109_FOW_TiO2_ETDandMS3_F3.raw\n20171013_F1_RM_WJP_0109_FOW_TiO2_ETDandMS3_F4.raw\n20171013_F1_RM_WJP_0109_REV_TiO2_ETDandMS3_F1.raw\n20171013_F1_RM_WJP_0109_REV_TiO2_ETDandMS3_F2.raw\n20171013_F1_RM_WJP_0109_REV_TiO2_ETDandMS3_F3.raw\n20171013_F1_RM_WJP_0109_REV_TiO2_ETDandMS3_F4.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nE-cadherin mechanotransduction activates EGFR/ERK signaling by inducing ligand shedding\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThe behavior of cells is governed by signals originating from their local environment, including mechanical forces that cells experience. Forces are transduced by mechanosensitive proteins, which can impinge on signaling cascades that are also activated by growth factor receptors upon ligand binding. However, the interplay between these mechanical and biochemical signals in the regulation of intracellular signaling networks remains incompletely understood. To elucidate this mechanochemical crosstalk, we conducted phosphoproteomic and transcriptomic analyses on epithelial cells subjected to mechanical strain. This approach revealed ERK signaling as a predominant hub activated in response to intercellular forces, initiated at the level of the upstream EGF receptor.  Strain-induced EGFR/ERK signaling relies on mechanosensitive E-cadherin adhesions, as it is disrupted upon genetic modulation of force transduction by the E-cadherin complex. Proximity labeling identified a connection between E-cadherin and ADAM17, an enzyme that mediates the shedding of soluble EGFR ligands. We developed a novel ADAM probe that showed that mechanical strain induces ADAM17 activity, which is essential for relaying forces to EGFR/ERK signaling. Collectively, our data demonstrate how forces transduced by E-cadherin adhesion modulate the presence of EGFR ligands to regulate downstream ERK activity. Our findings illustrate how intercellular forces and biochemical ligands can operate within a single, linear signaling cascade.\n\n[METHODS]\nMDCK cells were metabolically labeled with 14N212C6-lysine and 14N412C6-arginine (SILAC light) or 15N213C6-lysine and 15N413C6-arginine (SILAC heavy). Heavy label incorporation was confirmed to be at least 95% before experiments. Cells were plated onto stretch plates (light on non-stretched wells and heavy on stretched wells in the forward experiment, and the opposite for the reverse experiment), stretched for 30 minutes as described above and lyzed in buffer containing 8M urea, 1M ammonium bicarbonate (ABC), 10 mM tris(2-carboxyethyl)phosphine hydrochloride (TCEP), 40 mM 2-chloroacetamide (CAA), 1:50 (w/v) EDTA-free protease inhibitor cocktail (Roche, 12273700), 1% (v/v) phosphatase inhibitor cocktail 2 (Sigma, P5726), and 1% (v/v) phosphatase inhibitor cocktail 3 (Sigma, P0044). To obtain sufficient protein (~20 mg), 15 sequential runs of two 6-well plates were performed. All lysates were pooled, heavy and light SILAC-labelled samples were mixed and diluted 4-fold with 1 M ABC, upon which trypsin (Worthington) was added (20 ng/mg protein). After protein digestion for 18 hours at 37°C, the peptides were loaded on Sep-pak (Waters, 3CCTC18 cartridge). Titanium dioxide (TiO2) beads (ZirChrom) were prepared (10:1 beads:protein) by washing them with 5% NH3 (in H2O) using centrifugation, followed by loading solution (80% Acetonitrile, 6% TFA). The peptides were eluted from Sep-pak with loading solution and combined with the TiO2 beads. After a 5 minute incubation at 37°C, the beads were loaded on an in-house produced C8 stage-tip and the flow-through was discarded after centrifugation. After washing 3 times with Wash buffer (80% Acetonitrile, 1% TFA) and once with transfer buffer (80% Acetonitrile, 0.5% FA), phospho-peptides were eluted with 200 µl 5% NH3 in 30% ACN, into 40 µl 20% FA after which the sample volume was reduced in a SpeedVac concentrator to 20 µl. To acidify the solution, 20 µl of 10% FA was added, before loading the peptides on a C18 stage tip. Peptides were immediately fractionated offline using 5% ACN (pH=10), followed by 10% ACN (pH=10), followed by 50% ACN with 0.1% FA. The volumes of the fractions were reduced in a SpeedVac concentrator and samples were acidified with 0.1% FA. The peptides were separated by liquid chromatography on a 30 cm pico-tip column (75 µm ID, New Objective) with 1.9 µm aquapur gold C-18 material (dr. Maisch). The peptide mixtures were eluted using a 140 min. gradient (7% to 40%, ACN 0.1% FA), delivered by an easy-nLC 1000 (Thermo Scientific), and electro-sprayed directly into a Orbitrap Fusion Tribrid Mass Spectrometer (Thermo Scientific). Detection in ion trap was operated in data-dependent mode with a cycle time of 1 sec., in which the full scan over the 350-1,500 mass range was performed at a resolution of 240,000. Most intense ions (intensity threshold of 1.5 x104) were isolated by the quadrupole and then fragmented with a HCD collision energy of 30%. The maximum injection time of the ion trap was set to 50 milliseconds.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nCanis familiaris (dog) (canis lupus familiaris)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
