(K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:09317), DSPU (K,nterm), DSSBU (K,nterm),\n    DHSO (carbonyl-reactive D,E; AC=XLMOD:02226), BMSO (cysteine-specific; C),\n    SDASO (K,nterm; photo+MS-cleavable), SDASO-L (K,nterm; photo+MS-cleavable),\n    SSD (K,nterm), CBDPS (K,nterm), iQlnk (K,nterm)\n  PIR-type enrichable (reporter-ion, NOT backbone MS-cleavable): PIR (K,nterm), BDP-NHP (K,nterm)\n  Common non-cleavable: BS3/BS2G (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02000/02005), DSS (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02001), EDC (D,E,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02010), formaldehyde (K,nterm)\n  IMAC-enrichable (non-cleavable): PhoX (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02258), pDSPE (K,nterm; AC=null)\n  Photo-crosslinkers (non-cleavable, UV-activated): diazirine (K or incorporated aa), benzophenone (Bpa, pBpa; C or incorporated aa), SDA/sulfo-SDA (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02134/02136), aryl azide\n  NOTE: Do NOT extract proximity labeling enzymes (TurboID, BioID, APEX, biotin-phenol+H2O2) as crosslinkers — these are NOT chemical cross-linking reagents.\n\nterm: crosslinker concentration\nsdrf_column: comment[crosslinker concentration]\nontology: PRIDE:0000951\ndescription: Concentration of the crosslinking reagent used in the reaction.\npattern: number + mM or uM (e.g. "1 mM", "500 uM", "0.5 mM")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: crosslinker to protein ratio\nsdrf_column: comment[crosslinker to protein ratio]\nontology: PRIDE:0000952\ndescription: Molar ratio of crosslinker to protein in the reaction.\npattern: ratio expression (e.g. "50:1", "100-fold molar excess", "5 mM per 1 mg/mL")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: quenching reagent\nsdrf_column: comment[quenching reagent]\nontology: PRIDE:0000953\ndescription: Reagent used to stop, quench, neutralize, terminate, or consume the crosslinking reaction.\ninstructions:\n  Extract only if explicitly stated in the paper, PRIDE metadata, or cited protocol text.\n  Search for synonyms including: quenched, stopped, terminated, neutralized, blocked,\n  residual crosslinker, washed with, incubated with, reaction was halted.\nCommon quenchers:\n  - NHS ester crosslinkers such as BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU: Tris, ammonium bicarbonate, glycine, lysine\n  - Formaldehyde crosslinking: glycine or Tris are common, but only annotate if explicitly stated\nFor formaldehyde datasets:\n  - Inspect the 3–5 sentences after the formaldehyde crosslinking sentence.\n  - If the text says "as described previously" or "as described before", and no quencher is named, attempt to read the cited artical and extract the quenching reagent from its methods section.\n    If that fall back failes then return value=null with confidence="low" and reasoning="quenching reagent not explicitly stated in provided text; may require cited protocol."\n  - Do NOT infer glycine solely because formaldehyde was used.\n  - Do NOT treat PBS washes alone as a quenching reagent.\n  - Treat Tris-containing lysis buffer as a possible quencher only if the text clearly implies it was use\n  \nterm: crosslink distance\nsdrf_column: characteristics[crosslink distance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000948\ndescription: Maximum Cα–Cα distance constraint (spacer arm length) provided by the crosslinker.\npattern: number + Å (e.g. "11.4 Å", "30 Å", "~27 Å")\ninstructions: Extract the stated spacer length or max distance. Common values:\n  BS3/DSS ≈ 11.4 Å, DSSO ≈ 10.1 Å, DSBU ≈ 12.5 Å, EDC ≈ 0 Å (zero-length).\n  Return null if not explicitly stated in the text.\n\nterm: chemical cross-linking coupled with ms\nsdrf_column: comment[chemical cross-linking coupled with ms]\nontology: PRIDE:0000430\ndescription: MS-based cross-linking methodology identifier.\nallowed_values: cross-linking mass spectrometry\ninstructions: Always return "cross-linking mass spectrometry" for any XL-MS dataset.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null). For comment[cross-linker] the\n           "value" field should contain the full NT=...;AC=...;CL=...;TA=... string,\n           and also include separate sub-fields: "NT", "AC", "CL", "TA".\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null\n\nExample for comment[cross-linker]:\n{\n  "comment[cross-linker]": {\n    "value": "NT=DSSO;AC=XLMOD:02056;CL=yes;TA=K,S,T,Y",\n    "NT": "DSSO", "AC": "XLMOD:02056", "CL": "yes", "TA": "K,S,T,Y",\n    "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "crosslinked with 1 mM DSSO",\n    "evidence_location": "Methods"\n  }\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nE-cadherin mechanotransduction activates EGFR/ERK signaling by inducing ligand shedding\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThe behavior of cells is governed by signals originating from their local environment, including mechanical forces that cells experience. Forces are transduced by mechanosensitive proteins, which can impinge on signaling cascades that are also activated by growth factor receptors upon ligand binding. However, the interplay between these mechanical and biochemical signals in the regulation of intracellular signaling networks remains incompletely understood. To elucidate this mechanochemical crosstalk, we conducted phosphoproteomic and transcriptomic analyses on epithelial cells subjected to mechanical strain. This approach revealed ERK signaling as a predominant hub activated in response to intercellular forces, initiated at the level of the upstream EGF receptor.  Strain-induced EGFR/ERK signaling relies on mechanosensitive E-cadherin adhesions, as it is disrupted upon genetic modulation of force transduction by the E-cadherin complex. Proximity labeling identified a connection between E-cadherin and ADAM17, an enzyme that mediates the shedding of soluble EGFR ligands. We developed a novel ADAM probe that showed that mechanical strain induces ADAM17 activity, which is essential for relaying forces to EGFR/ERK signaling. Collectively, our data demonstrate how forces transduced by E-cadherin adhesion modulate the presence of EGFR ligands to regulate downstream ERK activity. Our findings illustrate how intercellular forces and biochemical ligands can operate within a single, linear signaling cascade.\n\n[METHODS]\nMDCK cells were metabolically labeled with 14N212C6-lysine and 14N412C6-arginine (SILAC light) or 15N213C6-lysine and 15N413C6-arginine (SILAC heavy). Heavy label incorporation was confirmed to be at least 95% before experiments. Cells were plated onto stretch plates (light on non-stretched wells and heavy on stretched wells in the forward experiment, and the opposite for the reverse experiment), stretched for 30 minutes as described above and lyzed in buffer containing 8M urea, 1M ammonium bicarbonate (ABC), 10 mM tris(2-carboxyethyl)phosphine hydrochloride (TCEP), 40 mM 2-chloroacetamide (CAA), 1:50 (w/v) EDTA-free protease inhibitor cocktail (Roche, 12273700), 1% (v/v) phosphatase inhibitor cocktail 2 (Sigma, P5726), and 1% (v/v) phosphatase inhibitor cocktail 3 (Sigma, P0044). To obtain sufficient protein (~20 mg), 15 sequential runs of two 6-well plates were performed. All lysates were pooled, heavy and light SILAC-labelled samples were mixed and diluted 4-fold with 1 M ABC, upon which trypsin (Worthington) was added (20 ng/mg protein). After protein digestion for 18 hours at 37°C, the peptides were loaded on Sep-pak (Waters, 3CCTC18 cartridge). Titanium dioxide (TiO2) beads (ZirChrom) were prepared (10:1 beads:protein) by washing them with 5% NH3 (in H2O) using centrifugation, followed by loading solution (80% Acetonitrile, 6% TFA). The peptides were eluted from Sep-pak with loading solution and combined with the TiO2 beads. After a 5 minute incubation at 37°C, the beads were loaded on an in-house produced C8 stage-tip and the flow-through was discarded after centrifugation. After washing 3 times with Wash buffer (80% Acetonitrile, 1% TFA) and once with transfer buffer (80% Acetonitrile, 0.5% FA), phospho-peptides were eluted with 200 µl 5% NH3 in 30% ACN, into 40 µl 20% FA after which the sample volume was reduced in a SpeedVac concentrator to 20 µl. To acidify the solution, 20 µl of 10% FA was added, before loading the peptides on a C18 stage tip. Peptides were immediately fractionated offline using 5% ACN (pH=10), followed by 10% ACN (pH=10), followed by 50% ACN with 0.1% FA. The volumes of the fractions were reduced in a SpeedVac concentrator and samples were acidified with 0.1% FA. The peptides were separated by liquid chromatography on a 30 cm pico-tip column (75 µm ID, New Objective) with 1.9 µm aquapur gold C-18 material (dr. Maisch). The peptide mixtures were eluted using a 140 min. gradient (7% to 40%, ACN 0.1% FA), delivered by an easy-nLC 1000 (Thermo Scientific), and electro-sprayed directly into a Orbitrap Fusion Tribrid Mass Spectrometer (Thermo Scientific). Detection in ion trap was operated in data-dependent mode with a cycle time of 1 sec., in which the full scan over the 350-1,500 mass range was performed at a resolution of 240,000. Most intense ions (intensity threshold of 1.5 x104) were isolated by the quadrupole and then fragmented with a HCD collision energy of 30%. The maximum injection time of the ion trap was set to 50 milliseconds.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
